      "content": "I see. So from an originalist perspective, the focus is strictly on the fixed historical meaning. But wouldn't an argument be made that the Framers understood the Constitution to be a framework that must account for technological and societal changes—like the difference between 18th-century muskets and modern firearms—to remain functional? How does an originalist respond to the idea that a rigid adherence to the past might actually render the Amendment's protections obsolete or impractical in a modern society?",
